{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hello, I’m Dr. Lee. I’ll be taking care of you today. Can you tell me what brought you to the hospital?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I woke up at 3 a.m. with chest pain that went down my left arm and leg. I took nitroglycerin and it helped a little, but the pain came back this morning."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Did you have any shortness of breath, sweating, or nausea with the pain?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, none of that. Just the pain. But my blood sugar was 500 when I woke up. I gave myself 15 units of insulin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:07:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s quite high. We’ve started checking your blood sugar and running some tests. We’ve also given you insulin to bring it down."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. But is the chest pain from my heart? I’ve had this before and my cardiologist said it’s not cardiac."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s right. Your EKG hasn’t changed from your previous ones, and we’ve checked your troponin levels three times — all normal. That means no heart attack."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:13:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what’s causing the pain then?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Given your history, it’s likely not from your heart. It could be musculoskeletal or related to nerves. Sometimes uncontrolled diabetes can cause nerve pain or gastroparesis, which might refer pain to the chest."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:17:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I do have chronic stomach pain, but no nausea this time. And I’ve been stressed — my son’s drinking is making things hard at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Stress can definitely worsen both pain and blood sugar. We’ll keep monitoring you, but we don’t think this is a cardiac emergency."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:21:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that’s a relief. But why was my blood sugar so high?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We don’t see signs of infection — your urine, chest X-ray, and physical exam are clear. You say you’ve been taking insulin as prescribed?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:25:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I have. But maybe I need a change in my dose?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We consulted the diabetes team. They suggest increasing your mealtime insulin to 20 units with each meal, on top of your 60 units at night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:29:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been using sliding scale for years. Is this different?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, this is a fixed dose with meals, plus the sliding scale if your sugar is high. It gives more consistent coverage. We’ll start it here so you can get used to it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I can try that. But I’m worried about low sugars."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:35:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll monitor closely. You’ll check your blood sugar before meals, and adjust based on how high it is — we’ll give you clear instructions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That makes sense. What about my omeprazole? I stopped it because I thought my insurance didn’t cover it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:39:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We checked — it is covered. We’d like you to restart it. It helps protect your stomach, especially since you have a history of ulcers."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, I didn’t know. I’ll try to fill it again. I have a GI appointment soon for my stomach pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:43:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. And we noticed you haven’t been taking aspirin. Is that true?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, the pharmacy said they didn’t have it. But I know I should be on it for my heart."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:47:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll send you home with instructions to buy it over the counter. It’s important for preventing clots, especially with your stent."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:49:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll do that. What about the chest pain? Should I still use nitroglycerin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:51:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, keep it with you. Use it if the pain comes on, but call us if it’s different — like if it spreads, gets worse, or you get short of breath."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:53:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. So this pain isn’t dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:55:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s uncomfortable, but not an emergency. We think it’s more related to nerves or muscles. Your cardiologist previously said it’s not from blocked arteries."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:57:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That matches what he said. Maybe I need to see a pain specialist?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2176-02-04 13:59:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We tried to set that up, but they need a referral from your primary doctor. When you see him, ask about it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2176-02-04 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thank you. I feel better knowing it’s not my heart."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs are stable, and your blood sugar is improving. We’d like to discharge you today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. What do I need to do at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Keep taking your insulin — 60 units at night, 20 units with each meal. Check your sugar before meals. If it’s over 150, add extra units as we discussed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So if it’s 250, I take 12 extra — that’s 32 total?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. And restart omeprazole daily — your insurance covers it. If you can’t get it, Prilosec OTC is the same."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And aspirin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, 325 mg daily — buy it over the counter. Keep all your follow-ups: primary care, cardiology, and GI."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. And if the chest pain changes?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Call your doctor or come back if it’s different — like more pressure, radiates to your jaw, or you get short of breath."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor. I feel much better already."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:20:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll get your discharge papers ready. You’re doing well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "One more thing — should I be worried about my son affecting my health?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:24:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Stress can definitely impact blood sugar and pain. Consider talking to a counselor or support group. Your doctor can help refer you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll think about that. Thanks for listening."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2176-02-05 09:28:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Of course. Take care, and we’ll see you in follow-up."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2176-02-04 12:40:00",
    "admission_end": "2176-02-05 17:30:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 60-year-old woman with a history of coronary artery disease, diabetes, hypertension, and hypercholesterolemia was admitted for left-sided chest pain radiating to the arm and leg, which woke her at 3 a.m. The pain was similar to prior episodes previously determined to be non-cardiac. Serial troponins were negative, EKGs showed no changes, and a chest X-ray revealed no acute abnormalities, ruling out acute cardiac or pulmonary causes. Her symptoms were attributed to likely musculoskeletal, neuropathic, or gastrointestinal origins. She presented with hyperglycemia (blood glucose 500), managed by adjusting her insulin regimen to 60 units glargine at bedtime and 20 units lispro with meals, plus sliding scale. She was restarted on omeprazole after clarification that her insurance covers it and advised to resume daily aspirin. She was discharged home in stable condition with follow-up arranged and clear instructions for medication adjustments, blood sugar monitoring, and when to seek care.",
    "problems": [
      "Other chest pain",
      "Coronary atherosclerosis of native coronary artery",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Asthma, unspecified type, unspecified",
      "Pure hypercholesterolemia",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty status"
    ]
  }
}
